\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A selected Gaussian background}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A \newvocab{selected Gaussian background} is a tuple
##{b=(\Pi,w,m,P),\qquad \Pi=(V,E,s,h),}
where #{\Pi} is a \vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002},
#{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}}, #{m>0}, and #{P} is an orthogonal
projector on the counting inner-product space #{H=\mathbb R^V}, with
##{P^2=P=P^{\dagger},\qquad 0<\operatorname{rank}P<|V|.}
The selected space is #{X=\operatorname{im}P}, its orthogonal
complement is #{Y=\ker P}, and the observed random vector is
#{P\phi\in X}. Incidence, the Green operator and the full law are
##{\begin{aligned}
(\extd\phi)(e)&=\phi(h(e))-\phi(s(e)),\\
Q&=\extd^\dagger W\extd+m^2\bbI>0,\qquad G=Q^{-1},\\
\phi&\sim\mu_Q.
\end{aligned}}
The weight operator multiplies pointwise,
#{(Wa)(e)=w(e)\,a(e)}. Its counting-basis matrix is the diagonal
of the values #{w(e)}, recorded in \ref{fdip-00LK}. The law
#{\mu_Q} uses the \vocabk{induced Euclidean volume}{fdip-002H}. These
are derived from the tuple, not additional choices.}

\p{Three constructions supply such projectors, provided their ranks
are nonzero and proper.}
\ol{
\li{A retained subset #{R\subsetneq V} gives
#{P_R\phi=\sum_{v\in R}\phi(v)\delta_v}. Identifying
#{\operatorname{im}P_R} with #{\mathbb R^R} recovers the
\vocabk{coordinate background}{fdip-000G}.}
\li{A \vocabk{finite signed graph symmetry}{fdip-004S}
#{\rho:\Gamma\to O(H)} extends to #{\mathbb R[\Gamma]}.
For #{e^2=e=e^{\dagger}}, where #{[g]^{\dagger}=[g^{-1}]},
#{P=\rho(e)} is orthogonal because
#{\rho(e)^{\dagger}=\rho(e^{\dagger})}. The [precision commutes](fdip-004T)
with every #{\rho(g)}, hence with this #{P}.}
\li{For a #{\Gamma}-stable subspace #{X\subset H}, its orthogonal
projector commutes with #{\rho(\Gamma)}: orthogonality makes
#{X^\perp} stable too. It need not commute with #{Q}. Within a
[repeated real isotypic block](fdip-0050), the chosen multiplicity
subspace may fail to be invariant under the positive multiplicity
operator #{Q_\Xi}. These are different constructions: a subrepresentation
projector commutes with #{\rho(\Gamma)} but need not commute with
#{Q}; a group-algebra projector commutes with #{Q} but need not
commute with #{\rho(\Gamma)}. Centrality of #{e} is sufficient
for the latter commutation, and hence for a stable image.}
}

\p{Every subspace has a unique orthogonal projector for the specified
metric. This makes the selector equivalent to choosing #{X}, without
choosing an additional complementary subspace. An oblique idempotent
#{T^2=T} also gives a Gaussian observation #{T\phi}, and orthogonal
conjugation does transport it naturally:
#{T\mapsto OTO^{-1}}. But it also records a possibly nonorthogonal
kernel, so the image alone no longer determines the observation or
its covariance #{TGT^{\dagger}}. General linear observations are outside this
definition. No physical rule selecting #{P} from an environment is
assumed.}
